WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.730
In this video, you take a snapshot of a volume, restore it to a new volume, and export the volume to an image for new virtual machines.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:15.040
On the Volumes page, open the action menu of the two gigabyte volume demo-data.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.630
Select Take Snapshot.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.500
Name the snapshot demo-data-snap,

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.250
and select Create.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.790
Open the volume to see its snapshots.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.900
Select the Snapshots tab.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:35.180
The snapshot is ready to use. Open its action menu.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.380
Select Restore.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:43.330
Restoring creates a new volume and leaves the original untouched. Name it demo-data-restored.

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:46.160
Keep the default storage class.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.590
Select Create.

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:55.140
On the Volumes page, the restored two gigabyte volume demo-data-restored sits next to the original.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:00.760
Next, export the original volume to an image. Open its action menu,

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.360
and select Export Image.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.950
Keep the namespace demo, and name the image demo-data-image.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:13.530
Choose the storage class for the image, here longhorn-v2.

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:17.750
Select Create to start the export.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:26.660
On the Images page, demo-data-image is active. You can now create new virtual machines from it, or download it.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:34.299
You now have a snapshot, a restored copy, and an image of the volume. Next, you control where virtual machines run.
